DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppnnmmccaatt reads one or more PNM images as input, concatenates them  either
       left to right or top to bottom, and produces a single PNM image as out‐
       put.

       The arguments are names of input files.  Any one of these, but not more
       than  one, may be "-" to indicate Standard Input.  If you have no argu‐
       ments, that means a single input image from Standard Input (and that is
       pointless - the output is the same as the input).

       To assemble a regular grid of images, you can use ppaammuunnddiiccee.

       To  do  the  reverse,  you  might use ppaammddiiccee to split an image up into
       smaller ones of equal size or ppaammccuutt to chop off part of  an  image  or
       extract part of an image.

       ppnnmmttiillee concatenates a single input image to itself repeatedly.


OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In  addition  to  the options common to all programs based on libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩  ),  ppnnmmccaatt  recognizes  the
       following command line options:


   DDiirreeccttiioonn
       --ttooppbboottttoomm, --ttbb
              Combine images vertically, top to bottom.


       --lleeffttrriigghhtt, --llrr
              Combine images horizontally, left to right.

              You must specify the direction by specifying one of the above.




   JJuussttiiffiiccaattiioonn
       --jjlleefftt, --jjcceenntteerr, --jjrriigghhtt
                If the PNM images are not all the same height when you're con‐
              catenating left
                to right or width when you're concatenating top to bottom, the
              smaller ones
                have  to  be  justified  with the largest.  By default, ppnnmmccaatt
              centers
                them, but you can specify justification to  one  side  or  the
              other with one of
                the --jj_x_x_x options.  So, --ttooppbboottttoomm --jjlleefftt would stack the PNMs
              on top of each other, flush  with  the  left  edge.   --lleeffttrriigghhtt
              --jjbboottttoomm  would line up the images left to right with their bot‐
              tom edges aligned as if sitting on a table.



   MMaarrggiinn CCoolloorr
       --wwhhiittee, --bbllaacckk
              These options specify what color to use to  fill  in  the  extra
              space  when  doing  the justification.  If neither is specified,
              ppnnmmccaatt chooses whichever seems to be right for the images.




